@meta
id: 21-10
title: Only the lines that match
chapter: 21
type: lesson
skills: macro-play, macro-record
par: 90
minutes: 8
vscode: find each ticket number, select it, wrap it in a link, paste the number again
open: CHANGELOG.md

@brief
Release notes are going on the public site and every ticket reference
has to become a link. A bullet that starts with a ticket number:

    - ORD-512 refund window is now 30 days

becomes

    - [ORD-512](https://jira.example.com/browse/ORD-512) refund window is now 30 days

The ticket number appears twice in the result, so this is a yank and a
put, not a substitute you would enjoy writing. Bullets without a ticket
number, and the headings, stay exactly as they are. The bullets are
spread across three releases.

Record the macro on the first ticketed line. Then let `:g` pick out
the other lines that need it and play the macro on each.

@keys
| `:g/pattern/normal @a` | play `a` on every line that matches |
| `yE` | yank to the end of the WORD, hyphen included |
| `E` | to the end of the WORD |

@hints
On one line: `^w` onto the ticket, `yE` to take it, `i[` `Esc`, `E`
to the end of it, `a](https://jira.example.com/browse/` `Esc`, `p`,
`a)` `Esc`.
--
The pattern is what a ticketed bullet looks like at the start of the
line: `^- ORD-`. Once a line is edited it starts `- [ORD`, so the line
you recorded on will not match twice.
--
`4j`, record, then `:g/^- ORD-/normal @a` and Enter.

@solution
    4j  qa ^w yE i[ <Esc> E a](https://jira.example.com/browse/ <Esc> p a) <Esc> q   :g/^- ORD-/normal @a<Enter>   :wq<Enter>

Seventy-eight keystrokes, nearly all of them the URL.

Chapter 15 gave you `:g//normal` with the keys written out on the
command line. That works until the keys include an `Esc` or two, and
then you are typing `Ctrl-V Esc` into a command and squinting at `^[`.
The macro version separates the two concerns. You record the edit
where you can see it working, and `:g` supplies only the pattern and
the word `normal @a`.

It is worth being deliberate about which tool does the selecting.
`99@a` with a search inside the macro is the right shape when the
targets are found by their content and the edit consumes the match.
`:g//normal @a` is the right shape when the targets are lines and you
can name them with a pattern. Here the pattern is easy and anchored,
and the macro's own search would have needed the same care about
wrap-around. Let `:g` do the finding.

@start CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## 2.14.0

- ORD-512 refund window is now 30 days
- Bumped tsyringe to 4.8
- ORD-498 cancelled orders keep their cancellation reason
- ORD-501 shipped orders can no longer be cancelled from the admin panel

## 2.13.0

- Node 20 in CI
- ORD-476 customer email is validated on create
- Dropped the legacy refund endpoint
- ORD-470 payment retries once on a gateway timeout

## 2.12.1

- ORD-455 fixed a double refund on concurrent cancellation
- Documentation for the seed script

@expected CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## 2.14.0

- [ORD-512](https://jira.example.com/browse/ORD-512) refund window is now 30 days
- Bumped tsyringe to 4.8
- [ORD-498](https://jira.example.com/browse/ORD-498) cancelled orders keep their cancellation reason
- [ORD-501](https://jira.example.com/browse/ORD-501) shipped orders can no longer be cancelled from the admin panel

## 2.13.0

- Node 20 in CI
- [ORD-476](https://jira.example.com/browse/ORD-476) customer email is validated on create
- Dropped the legacy refund endpoint
- [ORD-470](https://jira.example.com/browse/ORD-470) payment retries once on a gateway timeout

## 2.12.1

- [ORD-455](https://jira.example.com/browse/ORD-455) fixed a double refund on concurrent cancellation
- Documentation for the seed script
